  "query_preview": "..\n\nThrough next Monday...\n\nSatellite imagery shows an upper low over southern SK and the\nshortwave responsible for Monday's t-storm activity exited well\ninto the Dakotas. There remain areas of low cloud and perhaps even\nlocalized fog but this should erode early this morning as we see\ndrying from the NW. We will remain under a drier NW flow through\ntoday with seasonably cool temps courtesy of a lee side surface\nhigh. There could be a few light showers/sprinkles over the\nsouthern mountains per diurnal heating, but otherwise we are\nlooking at a dry day with temps in the low to mid 70s, after a\nchilly start. A weak shortwave arriving from the west will bring a\ngreater potential for showers/weak t-storms mainly over the\nmountains and foothills Wednesday. Most areas will again be dry,\nwith temps again in the low-mid 70s, limited by easterly winds.\n\nThings begin to get more interesting Thursday as a backed flow\nallows for a bit of moisture return followed by another shortwave.\nShear & instability seem to support a risk of severe storms on\nthis day, and this is something to monitor. Temps should be\nslightly warmer with highs in the 70s.\n\nFriday through the weekend, ensembles are in good agreement in\nshowing a significant low height anomaly forming over the PacNW\nand northern Rockies, with downstream blocking provided by a ridge\nover the Mississippi Valley thru central Canada. Cluster analyses\nshow remarkable agreement in the timing/depth of the developing \nlow. As a result, confidence is high for active weather (including\npotential severe t-storms Friday & Saturday), elevated chances of\nprecipitation, and cooler temps by Sunday & Monday. Details are \nhighly uncertain of course, but there is model consensus for \nkeeping the track of the low far enough north to prevent upslope \nprecip. Thus, this is looking more like waves of showers and \nt-storms, and eventually post-frontal wind of some magnitude in \nthe Sunday-Monday time frame. Ensembles show a fairly solid wind \nsignal on these days, greatest along western foothills. Finally, \nmid level temps look to cool enough to support high elevation snow\nover the Beartooth-Absaroka and Crazy Mountains by late in the \nweekend. Anyone with outdoor plans over the weekend, especially in\nthe high country, should keep tabs on the forecast.\n\nCurrent indications are for the low to fill and lift north\n(allowing for rising heights) next Monday. Regarding temps, Friday\nis looking like the warmest day (mid 70s to mid 80s) followed by\ncooling for the weekend, and below normal temps Sunday and Monday.\n\nJKL",
